From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] core0: a silent writer into the PSRAM window, and the evidence it works

The coherency experiment. Core 0 writes a header and an incrementing counter
into 0x3df00000..0x3e000000 every 100 ms, flushing with esp_cache_msync(),
and core 1 reads the same addresses through /proc/esp-shared (the kernel half
of this is patches/kernel/shared/final/esp_shared.c).

Three deliberate choices, each the reason the first two attempts failed:

  - It logs nothing. A high-volume log hook through the log pipe wedges this
    board a second after handoff, at exactly this stage -- ble_hid.c records
    the measurement. Every value goes into the window instead, so the Linux
    side is both the reader and the evidence and no logging is needed.

  - It allocates nothing. The firmware has no PSRAM heap
    (CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_MEMMAP=y, CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_MALLOC unset), so both
    earlier probes got NULL from heap_caps and returned before writing.

  - It gates itself on the MMU. The task polls MMU entry 0x1f0 until it reads
    MMU_ACCESS_SPIRAM | 0x70, which is what init_linux_mmap() leaves behind.
    Writing before that would hit a different physical page, because the plain
    IDF map puts the same virtual address elsewhere. Polling the table means
    no start-up ordering for anyone else to get wrong.

Apply from build/esp-hosted, then rebuild the app with ninja.

Measured on hardware 2026-11-10: the counter advanced every sample
(458 -> 477 -> 495 -> 514 -> 533) with magic 0x53484d57 intact. With the
reader's cache invalidate disabled the counter still advanced, so core 1 is
not serving stale lines -- it runs with no data cache, as the port claims.
Core 0's esp_cache_msync() is the only maintenance a shared buffer needs.

